## recalculated bill (Assumed Par Value Method)

⛔ **do NOT compute this by hand.** run the role's tested calculator and
paste its output. every figure that reaches Delaware must come from it,
per `rule.forbid.hand-computed-figures`.

```sh
rhx franchise.tax.delaware \
  --authorized <authorized shares> \
  --par        <STATED par value, e.g. 0.0001> \
  --issued     <issued shares> \
  --assets     <gross assets, Form 1120 Sch. L "total assets"> \
  --years      <number of delinquent years>
```

gather the four inputs first:

| field | value | where it comes from |
|-------|-------|---------------------|
| authorized shares | | certificate of incorporation |
| **stated par value** | $ | certificate of incorporation |
| issued shares | | stock ledger / board consents |
| gross assets | $ | Form 1120 Schedule L, per §503(i) |

⚠️ **gross assets is PER YEAR, and its year anchor is contested.** the table
above is one year. a back-filed set has one Schedule L figure **per year**, and
two primary sources point at different years once you are late:

| source | the anchor | a 2023 report submitted in 2026 |
|---|---|---|
| **§503(i)** — statute | the fiscal year that ends in the calendar year *prior to filing* | fiscal year **2025** 🚫 |
| ⭐ **the Division** — `corp.delaware.gov/frtaxcalc/` | the fiscal year that ends the calendar year *of the report* | fiscal year **2023** ✅ |

they agree for an **on-time** filer and come apart by the full width of the
delinquency. confirm the anchor before any figure is sworn to — it is question
**8** on `2.call.delaware.stone`, and the two quotes sit side by side in
`0.gather.position.stone` and `3.pay.taxes.stone`.

⚠️ **stated par value is required and is easy to skip.** §503(a)(2) splits
the authorized shares by whether their stated par sits **below** or
**at-or-above** the assumed par, and the two halves are computed
differently. the old three-line formula on this stone covered only the
first half — on a $1.00-par charter it **understates the tax by 2×**.

paste the calculator's output here, verbatim:

```
<paste the full `franchise.tax.delaware` output>
```

⭐ **record the SENSITIVITY line.** it states the gross-assets range over
which the answer holds. gross assets are provisional at this stage — a
bank balance or receivable often surfaces later — and the band answers
"how wrong can that figure be?" once, instead of a fresh re-run on every
revision. it is also the line you can say aloud to the clerk.

| | |
|---|---|
| governs | |
| annual tax | $ |
| **holds for gross assets up to** | $ |

### ⭐ then ASK what the band retires — this is the step that gets skipped

the line above is not a clerical record. **read it against your actual assets
and write down which of these two cases you are in**, because they hand you
different work:

| your gross assets vs. the band | what the Schedule L figure IS |
|---|---|
| **far below** it | ⭐ a **report-completeness** input under §503(b) — it decides what the form SAYS. **it cannot move the tax.** |
| near it, or above it | a **tax** input. the figure must be exact before any number is quoted |

⛔ **the trap is to carry "blocked on the gross-assets figure" as though it were
one blocker.** it is two, and they have different costs:

```
what does the form have to SAY?     <- §503(b) report completeness
what do we actually OWE?            <- §503(a)(2), and the band may already answer it
```

a charter whose assumed-par-value capital floors at the **§503(c)** minimum owes
the minimum for **every** assets value inside the band.

> 🚩 **this sentence said "the §503(a)(2) minimum" until a later pass, and the
> mis-cite has a SPECIFIC failure mode — I walked into it myself that day.**
>
> §503(a)(2) is the **method**; the floor is one subsection down, at **§503(c)**:
> *"in no case shall the tax … computed by paragraph (a)(2) of this section be
> more than $200,000 **nor less than $400**."*
>
> ⛔ follow the old cite and you land instead on §503(a)'s final sentence,
> which is a **PRORATION and reads like a floor**:
>
> > "Whenever the amount of the assumed par value capital … is **less than
> > $1,000,000**, the amount of the tax attributable thereto shall be the amount
> > that **bears the same relation to $400** that the amount of the assumed par
> > value capital bears to $1,000,000."
> >
> > — 8 Del. C. § 503(a), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html
>
> a 10,000,000-share charter at $0.00001 par has ~$100 of assumed par value
> capital, so that sentence yields a few **cents** — and a reader who stops
> there concludes the minimum is negligible rather than $400. the floor is real,
> it is statutory, and it is **not in the subsection that computes**.
>
> ⭐ this is the container rule in one page: §503(a) computes, §503(c) bounds,
> and a pull scoped to the computation returns a wrong answer that looks whole. if yours sits orders of
magnitude under the band's upper bound, **the second question is already
closed** and only the first is open. that is a much smaller gate than
"blocked", and it changes what you ask the clerk.

⚠️ **the federal return is still a precondition either way.** §503(i) defines
gross assets as those *"reported to the United States"* — past tense — so a year
with no return leaves the statutory term with no referent. **the return gates
the FORM, never the FIGURE.**

🚩 **and state the band's own limits.** it is computed from the inputs you
supplied. if the authorized-share count, the stated par, or the issued count is
itself provisional, the band is provisional too — re-run before you rely on it.

---

## ⛔ FIRST — which tax figure feeds the accrual? § 502(c) decides it

**what.** the number Delaware assessed is **not** the number you accrue on, if
you make the election. § 502(c) says so in its own first sentence:

> "If the annual franchise tax report and the franchise tax due are not filed or
> paid by the corporation as required by this chapter, the Secretary of State
> shall ascertain and fix the amount of the franchise tax as determined in the
> manner prescribed by § 503(a) of this title and the amount so fixed by the
> Secretary of State shall stand as the basis of taxation under the provisions of
> this chapter **unless the corporation shall thereafter elect to compute the
> franchise tax in the manner prescribed by § 503(a)(2) of this title by filing
> the annual franchise tax report and complying with the provisions of § 503(b)**
> of this title."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 502(c), first sentence
> · source of record: `refs/de-title8-ch5-franchise-tax.txt`

**why it is the first question on this page, not a later one.** every figure
below is computed from an annual tax. feed it the assessed Authorized-Shares
number and the accrual is arithmetically perfect and answers the wrong question
— by two orders of magnitude on a low-par charter.

⭐ **read the three words that do the work.**

| the words | what they settle |
|---|---|
| *"shall stand … **unless**"* | the assessment is a **default**, never a determination. it stands because no report was filed, not because it was found correct |
| *"shall **thereafter** elect"* | the election is expressly available **after** the Secretary has already fixed the amount. the sentence attaches no deadline to it |
| *"**and** complying with … § 503(b)"* | the report alone is not enough. § 503(b) wants issued shares **and** total gross assets, in one statement, at the time of filing |

⚠️ **so a clerk who asks for one of the two has not waived the other.** § 502(c)
routes the election through § 503(b), and § 503(b) makes the consequence of an
incomplete statement silent rather than loud — it does not reject the report, it
re-prices the year back onto (a)(1).

🚩 **this subsection has TWO sentences and they do opposite jobs.** the second
adds a **$200** penalty for a report not filed by March 1, and it is the one this
page used to cite. a pull scoped to "the § 502(c) penalty" returns a cost and
never the cure, and reads complete either way.

**how.** settle it before you run a figure below:

1. take the **assessed** annual figure from the bill
2. compute the **elected** figure — `rhx franchise.tax.delaware`, per the section above
3. accrue on the **elected** figure, and record both, so the delta is on the page
4. carry the § 503(b) pair — shares **and** assets — into every amended report

---

## total owed (estimated)

⛔ **do NOT hand-total this either.** the accrual has three parts that
move independently — a per-installment due-date structure (§504(a)), a
per-year penalty (§502(c)), and per-month interest that runs from **each**
due date (§504(c)). run:

```sh
rhx franchise.tax.delaware.accrue \
  --tax   <annual tax from above> \
  --years <comma-separated tax years, e.g. 2022,2023> \
  --asof  <YYYY-MM-DD>
```

⚠️ **once the annual liability reaches $5,000, March 1 is NOT the accrual
date.** §504(a) puts 40% on **June 1 of the tax year**, 20% September 1,
20% December 1, and the remainder on the next March 1. an accrual computed
off a single March 1 understates the interest badly, and the gap widens
with the assessment.

### 🚨 but WHICH $5,000 — and the answer is in §504(e), not §504(a)

the warning above says *"once the annual liability reaches $5,000."* **that
phrasing is loose in the one direction that costs you money**, and the statute
settles it two subsections down:

> "(a) The franchise tax shall be due and payable on March 1 following the close
> of the calendar year, except that with respect to a corporation whose
> franchise tax liability **for the current calendar year is estimated to be**
> $5,000 or more, a tentative return and tax shall be due and payable as
> follows: ..."
>
> "(e) The tentative return and tax paid thereon under subsection (a) of this
> section **shall be based on the annual franchise tax of the preceding year**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 504(a), (e),
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

⭐ **(a) says "estimated." (e) says what the estimate IS.** the quarterly
schedule is not keyed to the number Delaware later bills you for the current
year — it is keyed to **last year's annual franchise tax**, a figure already
fixed before the tax year opened.

#### 🚩 RETRACTED — the first write of that block quote was a PARAPHRASE

published 2026-09-14, corrected the same day. the §504(e) quote above first
read:

> ~~"... shall be based on the annual franchise tax of the year before."~~

the statute says **"of the preceding year."** the substitution was true in
substance and was still a **fabricated quotation**.

⛔ **the cause is worth more than the fix.** a house lint rule blocks the word
the statute actually uses, and the sanctioned escape for a verbatim quote is to
**retry the identical write** — never to reword. I reworded. **a tool
constraint is never a licence to alter quoted law**: a reader who checked this
against the Delaware Code would have found the quoted words absent, with no way
to tell a typo from an invention — and the whole point of a block quote is that
it survives that check.

⚠️ **this retraction is DELIBERATELY its own headed block.** the bad string has
to stay on the record, so an automated fabrication sweep will keep hitting it
forever. a hit under a 🚩 header is a **citation** of a corrected error; a hit
anywhere else is a live one. without the header the two are indistinguishable
and the sweep becomes a detector nobody trusts.

### why that is worth real money here

the two readings produce different bills on the same facts:

| what anchors the estimate | the back-year consequence |
|---|---|
| the **assessed** figure (method A) | over $5,000 ⇒ tentative returns were due **June 1 / Sept 1 / Dec 1 of each tax year** ⇒ §504(c) interest at 1½% per month runs from those dates |
| ⭐ the **recalculated** figure (§503(a)(2) election) | under $5,000 ⇒ **no tentative return was ever due** ⇒ no obligation to be delinquent on ⇒ interest runs from **March 1 only** |

so the same recalculation that drops the tax also drops **three accrual start
dates per year**, across every delinquent year. that is a second, independent
saving, and it is invisible if you read §504(a) alone.

⛔ **and §504(f) closes the loop on the penalty side:**

> "(f) The penalties for nonpayment of the tentative franchise tax as set forth
> in subsection (a) of this section shall be the same as those applied for any
> nonpayment of franchise tax in this title."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 504(f)

⭐ read it for what it does **not** say: it supplies a **rate**, never an
**obligation**. a penalty for nonpayment of a tentative tax has no obligation to
attach to where no tentative tax was ever due.

### ⚠️ do NOT settle this by reason — two things are genuinely open

the argument above is clean and it rests on two facts this route does not hold:

1. **does the recalculation reach back for §504(e) purposes?** Delaware could
   anchor on the tax **as assessed at the time**, not as later corrected. no
   part of §504 answers that, and a guess here closes a gate that should stay
   open.
2. 🚩 **what was the annual franchise tax of the year BEFORE the first
   delinquent year?** §504(e) is a chain, and the chain needs a base. for a
   2022 report the anchor is **2021's** annual franchise tax — a figure that
   predates the void and is **not on file here**. if 2021 was itself billed at
   method-A rates and never corrected, the 2022 anchor may sit above $5,000
   even after the election.

🎯 **both are call questions, not research questions.** they are in
`2.call.delaware.stone` as question 13. until they are answered, run the accrual
**both ways** and carry the spread as a range.

🎯 **if Delaware has already issued a bill, test it:**

```sh
rhx franchise.tax.delaware.accrue --tax <...> --years <...> --fit <their total>
```

`--fit` searches for the accrual date that reproduces the State's figure
and reports the residual. that is the only way to tell a correct
assessment from a wrong one — and the only way to learn **which tax
years** their number actually covers, which the rest of this route
depends on.

| item | Authorized Shares | Assumed Par Value |
|------|-------------------|-------------------|
| back taxes (from `franchise.tax.delaware`) | $ | $ |
| years owed | | |
| penalty + interest (from `.accrue`) | $ | $ |
| annual report fee × years owed | $ | $ |
| Certificate of Revival | $189.00 | $189.00 |
| **total** | $ | **$** |

> 🚩 **the annual-report-fee row was added 2026-09-02** — the table used to have
> four line items and none of them covered it. `franchise.tax.delaware` prints
> `⬅️ tax only` and `.accrue` returns penalty and interest, so **neither tool
> produces this figure** and its absence is silent in both.
>
> it is owed per year because the reports are owed per year: *"each missed year
> requires a separate annual report"* — `delaware.franchise.tax.delinquency.md`,
> step 2, and § 510 voids on an unfiled complete report independently of the tax.
>
> ⚠️ **do not enter $50 × years from this note.** $50 is the fee for a *current*
> non-exempt domestic report ($25 exempt), quoted from `corp.delaware.gov/frtax/`;
> whether Delaware charges the same on a **back** report is **not established in
> this repo**. take the per-year figure from the state on the step-2 call, and
> record which it quoted.

✅ **the $189.00 was re-checked 2026-08-31 and it HOLDS** — against Delaware's
live [fee schedule](https://corpfiles.delaware.gov/Fee_Schedule/AugustFee2026.pdf)
(rev. 2026-08-01), row `Revival (plus taxes to renew)`.

⭐ **if the corporation is EXEMPT, the fee is `$5.00`** — row `Revival (exempt)`.
check which one applies before you enter a figure; the gap is 38×.

> 🚩 **the flag this row used to carry was right, and its re-check url was not.**
> it read *"re-check `corp.delaware.gov/fee`"* — but that url is a **hub**, not
> the schedule. it pulls at 1,150 chars with **zero dollar figures** on it, so
> an agent who followed the instruction would have found no number to check
> against, and had no way to tell whether that meant "unchanged" or "not
> found."
>
> ⚠️ ~~**the vintage suspicion was also correct**: `renewalvoid16.pdf` is
> stamped `rev. 08/16` and states **$169.00**. the form is stale by $20~~ — see
> the correction below.
>
> ⛔ **a flag that names a re-check url owes the same duty as any citation: the
> url has to be one that can answer the question.** see
> `rule.require.bhrowser-citations`.

> 🚨 **correction — the form is NOT stale. the URL was.**
>
> `renewalvoid16.pdf` was **constructed, never harvested**, and it serves a
> `rev. 08/16` revision. Delaware's current form is **`rev. 08/2023`** and states
> the same **$189.00** the schedule does:
>
> > "The fee to file the Certificate is **$189.00**."
> > — `corpfiles.delaware.gov/Corp_Forms/Revival%20void.pdf`, harvested by nav
>
> **the navigation that earns it:**
>
> ```
> corp.delaware.gov/corpforms/  ->  "DOCUMENT TYPE"
>   -> corp.delaware.gov/formsdoctype09/  ->  "Revivals"
>     -> corp.delaware.gov/renew09/  ->  Corporation / Revival for Void
>       -> corpfiles.delaware.gov/Corp_Forms/Revival%20void.pdf
> ```
>
> ⭐ **so the row above was right for a reason it never stated.** the fee schedule
> IS the authority — but the form did not disagree with it, and the whole
> "stale by $20" account came from a **guessed url that succeeded at an old
> revision**. `rule.forbid.guessed-urls`
>
> ⚠️ note that the `16` in the slug is the revision stamp. **the url announced
> its own vintage** and three rounds of correction read past it.

